---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How the ABS Dashboard computes challenge success rates, Win Probability Added, and other metrics. Data sources, caveats, and known limitations."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-2">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-10">
      How the data is fetched, what the metrics mean, and where to be skeptical.
    </p>

    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8">
      <section aria-labelledby="data-sources-heading">
        <h2 id="data-sources-heading" class="text-xl font-display font-bold">Data Sources</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-slate-700">
          <li>
            <strong>Baseball Savant / ABS endpoint</strong> — Primary source for challenge counts,
            overturn decisions, and which party initiated each challenge. Updated daily.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>pybaseball / Statcast</strong> — Pitch-by-pitch data used to cross-reference
            challenge outcomes and compute pitch-level context.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>MLB Stats API</strong> — Team standings, game results, and win percentage.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="metrics-heading">
        <h2 id="metrics-heading" class="text-xl font-display font-bold">Key Metrics</h2>

        <div class="mt-4 space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold">Challenge WPA (Win Probability Added)</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-700 mt-1">
              For each successful challenge, we record the change in Win Probability from before to
              after the overturned call. WPA is summed across all successful challenges for a team
              or player. This is the headline metric — it captures not just whether a team wins
              challenges, but whether they win <em>important</em> challenges.
            </p>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mt-2 text-sm italic">
              Caveat: WPA is highly context-dependent and can swing dramatically on a single at-bat.
              We surface confidence intervals and sample size warnings throughout the site.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold">Success Rate</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-700 mt-1">
              Overturned challenges ÷ total challenges. Simple, but misleading without context — a
              team with 2/3 challenges is not better than a team with 40/70 challenges.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold">Net Overturns</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-700 mt-1">
              Overturns in a team's favor minus overturns against them. A team could have a high
              success rate but negative net overturns if they challenge rarely while their opponents
              challenge frequently.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="caveats-heading">
        <h2 id="caveats-heading" class="text-xl font-display font-bold">Caveats & Known Issues</h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-slate-700">
          <li>
            The 2026 season is the first with ABS at scale. Sample sizes are small through mid-season.
            We will add rolling confidence intervals as the season progresses.
          </li>
          <li>
            Not all challenge outcomes are available in real time. Data may lag by 24–48 hours after
            late-night games.
          </li>
          <li>
            Win Probability models differ across sources. We use a standard Statcast WPA model, which
            is not the same as ESPN or Baseball Reference.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="pipeline-heading">
        <h2 id="pipeline-heading" class="text-xl font-display font-bold">Pipeline</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-700 mt-4">
          Data is fetched daily via a GitHub Actions workflow, validated against strict Pydantic
          schemas, and committed as static JSON files. The site is rebuilt and redeployed on every
          commit via Cloudflare Pages. There is no runtime server — all data is baked in at build time.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-700 mt-3">
          The full pipeline source is available on GitHub. See <code>scripts/README.md</code> for
          instructions to run it locally.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
